*.tgz

# dotenv — but keep the template, or nobody can work out what the build needs.
# The values themselves must never be committed: PUBLIC_CV_PASSWORD ends up in
# the public JS bundle (recovered.md#R8), so a leaked .env is a leaked password.
.env*
!.env.example

# Astro build output and generated types.
# Both were committed until this file was corrected; they are reproducible from
# `npm run build` and there is no reason for 100 files of output in history.
dist/
.astro/

# NOTE: there is deliberately NO `public/` rule here.
#
# The old Gatsby .gitignore carried a bare `public`, which meant for Gatsby's
# build directory. Astro uses public/ for STATIC ASSETS - it is where every
# recovered project image lives (rebuild/public/images/, 53 files) along with
# fonts, icons, robots.txt and the web manifest. That rule was silently excluding
# all of it: the images had never been committed, and the only copies in git were
# the build-output duplicates under dist/. Ignoring dist/ without removing this
# line would have taken the portfolio's entire image set out of version control.

# Gatsby leftovers, kept until the legacy tree is deleted
.cache/

# Mac files
.DS_Store

# Yarn
yarn-error.log
.pnp/
.pnp.js
.yarn-integrity

# Vercel / Zeit
.now
.vercel